DOC -- documentation for the freeware commands==============================================    man <name>finds and shows everything here about a program; `man -k <word>' findsprograms by what they do.  README-DOCS has the rest.One subdirectory per package, holding whatever documentation it shippedwith: manual pages, .doc files, release notes, the upstream README.  Thefiles are plain OS-9 text, readable with `less' as well.Where a package is under the GPL, its COPYING file is kept here too -- thatis a licence obligation for the binaries in CMDS, not a convenience.Two exceptions worth knowing: gcc139 carries its licence insidegcc.texinfo rather than a separate file, and sbprolog is under SUNY StonyBrook's own licence (DOC/sbprolog/COPYING), not the GPL.The index files, which are not package directories:  CATEGORIES   every program grouped by what it is FOR -- start here if               you do not yet know the name you want  INDEX        what every program IS, alphabetically, with its caveats  ORIGINS      which archive each program came out of  README-KSH   why ksh could not be typed at, and what fixes it  USAGE        what every program says when asked for its help: the               command that asked, and the whole answer.  Captured               from the programs, not composed  DEPENDS      what each program needs besides its own binary  WHATIS       one line for every program -- `man -k' searches it  DOCS         every program's documents and source -- `man' reads it  MANPAGES     the manual pages on this disk, and where each one is  README-CIO   the starred programs, and how to supply cio  README-RUNNING   three ways to arrange /dd and /h0  README-ERRMSG    the error message file, and BASIC09's numbersThe package directories:  bash/     Bourne-Again Shell -- readme, OSK port notes  ed/       GNU ed 0.2 -- README, OSK port notes, ed.1, COPYING (GPL)  elvis/    Elvis 1.7 (vi clone) -- elvis/ctags/fmt/ref man pages + .doc            The program is CMDS/elvis, its source SRC/elvis; view and            vi.elvis are elvis under other names (DOC/README-VI).  find/     find 1.1.5 -- find.doc, find.man, release notes  gcc/      GCC 2.5.6 OS-9 port notes  grep/     GNU grep 2.0 -- README, OSK port notes, grep.man, COPYING (GPL)  ncurses/  ncurses -- installation, terminal and compile notes  sc/       sc spreadsheet -- manual and tutorial  sh/       Bourne shell v7.5 -- sh.doc, changelog, SCRIPTS/ examplesProvenance and per-command test status for everything in CMDS is in../SOURCES.txt.Docs extracted from the original .lzh archives kept in CMDS/archives/ --those archives also contain full C source for ed, elvis and grep, which isnot unpacked here.Files in this directory that are not per-package documentation:  INDEX                what is on this disk, section by section  ORIGINS              which archive each added program came from  STATUS               per-command test status  USAGE                captured usage text for the undocumented  EFFO-INFO            the EFFO forum disks' own info files, which carry                       each author's licence conditions -- read before                       redistributing anything from those disks  README-RUNNING       how to start things, and what to set first  README-DOCS          every way to find out how to use something  README-CIO           the starred programs need Microware's cio --                       three ways to set that up  DEPENDS              per program, every file it needs besides its own                       binary; `keep' reads it  README-FLOATINGPOINT math.l and math881.l give the same answers; link                       math881.l for a machine with a 68881  README-SRC-LS        SRC/ls, the source the ls on this disk is built from,                       and what it needed for OS-9  README-GCC           the gcc toolchains need setup and your own SDK;                       read it before trying to compile anything  README-VI            which vi to take, and what each needs  README-KERMIT        which kermit to take, and the two flags that decide                       whether a transfer works  README-GREP          ways to search a file, and which to take  README-ARCHIVERS     archivers by format -- which to pack with, which to                       unpack with  README-EDITORS       the editors that are not vi, and which to take  README-SHELLS        the five shells, and why SYS/login names ksh  README-NEWS          three news systems -- UUCPbb's, C News's tools and                       MNews with nn -- which to take, and the one                       `rnews' they cannot share  README-NETPBM        the netpbm image converters, and how to see them work  README-KEEP          taking part of this collection onto your own media  README-NAMES         programs here that share a name with a utility you                       already have -- which, and what decides which runs  README-MODULES       not everything in CMDS is a command -- drivers,                       descriptors and trap libraries live there too  README-BUSERR        programs that fault, and the trap of testing them                       bare  README-FORTRAN       the RTF/68K Fortran-77 system, and the one thing it                       needs  README-METAFONT      the eight bitmap-font tools, the base that now                       ships, and why no .pk file does  README-DVI           eleven DVI drivers, one per printer -- which to                       take, what each writes, and the font warning  README-NOT-SHIPPED   programs found in the archives that stayed out,                       why, and where each one is  README-X68K          the two programs for the Sharp X68000's window                       system, and what their Japanese documents say  NEEDCIO-INDEX        the older per-program notes on the starred programs